# Leapfrog and velocity Verlet take the same steps: the two differ in what
# they store. The log is the same, row by row, although the stored
# velocities of leapfrog are half a step behind the positions.
#
# RUN: sed -e 's|Inputs/|%S/Inputs/|' %S/mixture.toml > %t.verlet.toml
# RUN: sed -e 's|"VELOCITY_VERLET"|"LEAPFROG"|' %t.verlet.toml > %t.leapfrog.toml
# RUN: mdir run --overwrite %t.verlet.toml | grep "^INFO" > %t.verlet.log
# RUN: mdir run --overwrite %t.leapfrog.toml | grep "^INFO" > %t.leapfrog.log
# RUN: diff %t.verlet.log %t.leapfrog.log
# RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.leapfrog.log
# RUN: mdir emit %t.leapfrog.toml | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MODULE

# CHECK:      INFO:         0         0.0000
# CHECK:      INFO:       200         0.8000 {{ *}}-406.5155 {{ *}}-460.4736 {{ *}}53.9581

# Leapfrog carries the forces of the positions, as velocity Verlet does,
# and evaluates once per step, at its end. The step of energy returns the
# velocities of the time of the new positions, half a kick ahead of those
# that it stores, and the kinetic energy takes them.
#
# MODULE:      dyn.program @step(%x: !vec, %v: !vec, %f: !vec, %m: !real,
# MODULE:      attributes {velocity_offset = -0.5,
# MODULE:      %v1 = dyn.kick %v, %f, %m, %dt : !vec
# MODULE:      dyn.return %x1, %v1, %f1 : !vec, !vec, !vec
# MODULE:      dyn.program @step_energy(
# MODULE:      %v2 = dyn.kick %v1, %f1, %m, %half : !vec
# MODULE-NEXT: dyn.return %x1, %v1, %f1, %u1, %w1, %v2
# MODULE:      %xl, %vl, %fl, %u, %w, %vn = dyn.step @step_energy(
# MODULE:      %k = md.sum_particles gather(%vn, %m : !vec, !real)
# MODULE:      %g = md.sum_particles gather(%fl, %m : !vec, !real)
